<GuideLayout title="When the Lowest Unit Price Is Not the Whole Decision" description="Use unit price alongside shelf life, storage, cash outlay, product differences, coupon restrictions, and your household's actual usage.">
<p>Unit price answers one useful question: which option costs less for the same measured amount? It does not decide whether the amount is useful to you. A complete shopping decision can include timing, storage, cash available today, and whether the products really substitute for one another.</p>
<h2>The larger amount may not be fully used</h2><p>A 32 oz product at $5.12 costs $0.16/oz, while 16 oz at $3.20 costs $0.20/oz. But if eight ounces of the larger size spoil, its effective cost is $5.12 ÷ 24 usable ounces, about $0.213/oz. The package was cheaper per purchased ounce and more expensive per used ounce.</p><p>This is not an argument against bulk buying. It is a reason to compare the quantity with your actual routine. The <a href="/usage-tracker/">Usage Tracker</a> can replace a guess with your own completed usage periods.</p>
<h2>Storage and checkout cash are separate constraints</h2><p>A case can have the best per-item price and still not fit the available shelf, freezer, or vehicle space. Extra containers or rearrangement have practical costs even if they do not appear on the receipt.</p><p>The lower unit-price option can also require a higher payment today. Buying 24 items for $18 may beat six for $5 per item, but $18 is still more cash than $5. Unit price measures value per amount, not affordability at this trip.</p>
<h2>Products may not be interchangeable</h2><p>Two detergents can use different doses. Paper rolls can differ in sheet dimensions. A concentrated product may deliver more uses from less volume. Ingredients, fit, durability, taste, or compatibility may make a nominally similar product unsuitable. Choose the measure that best reflects the use, and avoid forcing a numeric comparison when the products solve different problems.</p>
<h2>Promotion restrictions can change the price</h2><p>A coupon may require a specific size, loyalty account, minimum quantity, or eligible variety. “2 for $X” may or may not allow one item at half the advertised total. Use the price and quantity you actually qualify to purchase. The <a href="/coupon-comparator/">Coupon & Discount Comparator</a> compares final prices after an eligible sale and coupon; it does not verify store terms.</p>
<h2>Shelf labels can use different bases</h2><p>A smaller number on a shelf tag is not necessarily cheaper when one price is per ounce and another is per pound, or one is per 100 g and another per kilogram. Normalize both first. The <a href="/guides/mixed-unit-comparisons/">mixed-unit guide</a> shows the conversions and the measurements that should remain separate.</p>
<h2>Buying extra helps only when the supply is useful</h2><p>A stock-up sale has two quantities: the money saved versus today’s normal price and the time represented by the extra supply. Knowing that each package saves $3 is more informative when you also know whether it adds two weeks or six months of stock.</p><p>Use the <a href="/stock-up-calculator/">Stock-Up Calculator</a> to show both. It will not tell you what you should buy; it exposes the economics and duration so you can weigh them against shelf life, storage, and cash available.</p>
</GuideLayout>
